{"acceptance_length": 6.671428571428572, "acceptance_lengths": [4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 15, 5, 7, 15, 8, 5, 2, 11, 6, 2, 5, 4, 8, 16, 1, 5, 7, 1, 2, 4, 10, 7, 2, 1, 6, 2, 3, 1, 5, 5, 1, 2, 7, 2, 4, 3, 1, 2, 6, 12, 16, 7, 6, 1, 16, 8, 2, 5, 5, 8, 13, 14, 1, 11, 14, 16, 16, 16, 14, 16, 4, 13, 3, 2, 7], "allocated_memory_after_bytes": 23321325568, "allocated_memory_before_bytes": 23321308160, "benchmark": "gsm8k", "completion": "We are given the following information:\n\n- Both pools have **leaks** that empty them at **4 gallons per minute**.\n- **4 minutes ago**, the **big pool had twice as much water as the small pool**.\n- **Now**, the **big pool has four times as much water as the small pool**.\n- We are to find **how much water the small pool has now**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Define Variables\n\nLet:\n- $ S $ = amount of water in the **small pool now** (in gallons)\n- $ B $ = amount of water in the **big pool now** (in gallons)\n\nWe are told:\n- $ B = 4S $ (now, the big pool has 4 times as much water as the small pool)\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Use the information from 4 minutes ago\n\nLet\u2019s find how much water was in each pool **4 minutes ago**.\n\nSince both pools are leaking at **4 gallons per minute**, in 4 minutes, each pool has **lost**:\n\n$$\n4 \\text{ minutes} \\times 4 \\text{ gallons/minute} = 16 \\text{ gallons}\n$$\n\nSo, 4 minutes ago:\n- The **small pool** had $ S + 16 $ gallons\n- The **big pool** had $ B + 16 $ gallons\n\nWe are told that **4 minutes ago**, the **big pool had twice as much water as the small pool**:\n\n$$\nB + 16 = 2(S + 16)\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Substitute $ B = 4S $ into the equation\n\n$$\n4S + 16 = 2(S + 16)\n$$\n\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n4S + 16 = 2S + 32\n$$\n\nNow solve for $ S $:\n\n$$\n4S - 2S = 32 - 16 \\\\\n2S = 16 \\\\\nS = 8\n$$\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{8} \\text{ gallons}\n$$\n\nSo, the **small pool has 8 gallons of water now**.", "decode_seconds": 2.5474185892380774, "draft_calls": 